the 2008 horror film the strangers has  become a disturbing classic and an  excellent example of the home invasion  genre of horror lock the door and join  us as we explore the meaning of the  film's dark nihilistic ending  kristen and james ride in uncomfortable  silence it's a cool night in february  and the pair are making their way from a  friend's wedding reception to james's  summer home james was so sure that  kristen was going to accept his marriage  proposal kristen's tear-stained face  says it all where do they go from here  there's a knock on the door a blonde  woman bathed in shadow asks for tamara  after an awkward exchange they send her  on her way james attempts to gather his  thoughts and leaves on a night drive  leaving kristin alone in the house then  suddenly another knock more insistent  this time  kristen grabs a knife and starts  charging her phone the landline is dead  and she can't shake the feeling that  someone is in the house so begins the  strangers which barrels forward with  unrelenting energy pitting the  emotionally strained couple against  three masked assailants james returns to  find kristen a mess and he soon finds  out why together they attempt to survive  in their own home defend themselves and  call for help all to no avail at the end  of their terrifying night james is  missing and kristen is badly injured her  attempts to signal for help from a  remote radio are a bust and upon  returning to the house the power cuts  out limping and surrounded by the masked  intruders kristen attempts to find james  only to be knocked out by the towering  male in the group she awakens tied to a  chair next to james distraught and  defeated with one searing question  why  kristin wakes up to the sound of pinup  girl opening the drapes warm sunlight  floods the room kristen grunts against  her tight restraints kicking and  whimpering as her three masked  assailants stand silently over her and  the defeated james we tend to expect  horrific things to take place under the  cover of darkness things that slink  creep and strike while we're sleepy  vulnerable and alone evolutionary  psychologists have theorized that our  fear of the dark lingers from the days  when human beings weren't at the top of  the food chain when nocturnal predators  were a real threat horror films take  advantage of our instinctual fear of the  dark allowing our imaginations to run  wild and conjure up worst case scenarios  in genre cinema horrific things tend to  lurk in the shadows that said there's no  shortage of horror film baddies that  work in the light the man eating shark  and jaws didn't confine its reign of  terror to the shadows and neither do the  trio of killers in the strangers and  this is precisely what makes the climax  of their murderous assault so disturbing  they open the curtains before killing  kristin and james unafraid and  unconcerned with the possibility that  some prying neighbor might see the fact  that the stranger's ends bathed in  sunlight is both unexpected and  terrifying further cementing the film's  core theme that random acts of violence  are exactly that random this isn't the  kind of evil that is miraculously  banished by the sun  there are few glimmers of hope  throughout the strangers one of them is  when mike played by dennis reynolds  himself glenn howerton arrives at the  house in response to james's upset voice  message mike's a good pal his friend's  marriage proposal fell through and here  he is at god knows what hour to pick up  the pieces unfortunately mike's good  intentions lead to his demise waltzing  into the house oblivious of the  situation he shot in the face by james's  itchy trigger finger to be fair when a  person blows their best friend's face  off the first reaction isn't typically  to steal his cell phone even as an  audience member it's easy to forget that  mike has a phone that could be kristin  and james's chance to contact the police  after the incident james announces that  he's going to try and call for help not  by checking his dead friend's pockets  but by venturing out for the two-way  radio in the woods to be fair the movie  wants us to forget that mike has a cell  phone so that it can make a dramatic  appearance in the final act bleeding out  on the floor kristen hears mike's phone  ring something we didn't even realize  was there  upon coming to lash to a chair kristen  breathlessly asks the trio what we've  all been wondering why are you doing  this to us  because you were home this attack has  nothing to do with kristen and james's  relationship struggles with the apparent  wealth of james's family or with any  sinister occult purpose they answered  the door it's just that random and  simple indeed the scariest thing about  the strangers isn't the masks the jump  scares or the bloodshed instead the film  offers a glimpse into the terrifying  notion that sometimes bad things happen  just because there's no grand reveal and  no explanation for our villains actions  when the assailants remove their masks  kristen and james don't recognize their  faces the killers are as the film title  suggests just strangers  this hits especially hard in these final  moments even at the film's end there are  no answers all we're left with is pure  dread because we know so little about  these anonymous boogeymen they are able  to remain just that something that can  go bump in the night without any warning  or purpose the conclusion isn't here to  satisfy your curiosity  satisfied with the damage they've  inflicted our now unmasked trio exit  james's summer house and pile into their  pickup their truck passes two mormon  boys walking their bikes on the side of  the road they stop and chat with the  boys then continue down the road as they  drive away pinup girl makes a curious  comment to dollface she says  it'll be easier next time the internet  loves theorizing and passionate  redditors believe pinup's comment may  imply that the events we see in the film  were an initiation right of sorts for  dollface indeed if you look at the  trio's masks and their credited names a  sort of hierarchy emerges man in mask  never speaks he's a dominating presence  who towers over his two smaller peers  pinup girl always appears assured and  well practiced with a name suggesting a  degree of maturity dollface meanwhile is  another story a weak voice beneath a  wide-eyed childish mask it's possible  that she's new to this this  interpretation potentially puts pinup  girl's comment that it will be easier  next time into context dollface's  struggle with the acts she's committed  is further implied by her interactions  with the mormon boys she's a sinner and  she's a little shaken up by what she's  just done but next time it won't be her  first rodeo  the idea for the strangers came from a  number of different real-life sources  while you have to go digging through the  film's bonus features and production  notes to uncover how writer director  brian burtino's own brush with home  invasion inspired the film the ending of  the strangers hammers home some of the  film's more well-known sources as our  unmasked trio pile into their pickup  truck the connective tissue to the  famous tate labianca murders is  unavoidable as bertino recounted in an  interview the film was indeed in direct  conversation with the murders enacted by  the manson family bertino explained that  learning about the story of charles  manson and his followers along with the  group's seeming lack of any singular  motivation had a massive impact on him  and his understanding of the minds of  killers writer brit hayes notes another  source of inspiration for the film this  incident is known as the kitty cabin  murders an unsolved quadruple homicide  that took place in a california railroad  town in 1981 one of the most unnerving  similarities between the strangers and  the kitty cabin murders takes place  during the film's conclusion with  kristen and james bound to chairs in  their living room and stabbed in the  early morning the scene bears a strong  resemblance to the fates of some of the  case's victims  love him or hate him jump scares are an  essential part of horror films while not  all screamers as they're sometimes  called are created equal some are nobler  than others the word stingers describes  the jump scare that gives the audience  one last jolt before the credits roll  and believe it or not it's a  time-honored tradition there's the dream  sequence at the end of carrie for  instance where our witchy heroine's  bloodied arm bursts out of her rocky  grave to snatch the traumatized suit and  who could forget the final shot of the  franchise originator friday the 13th  when jason's decomposing corpse springs  out of crystal lake for one final shock  a final jump scare is a totally  acceptable if a bit silly way to end a  horror film the strangers keeps that  tradition alive as the two mormon boys  who are going to need some serious  therapy after this venture into the  house they spy james and kristen's  mangled bodies and investigate hovering  over kristen the boys take in the crime  scene with a frankly eerie silence dumb  struck perhaps at the gruesome bloodbath  before them one boy hovers over  kristen's body extending his hand  towards her face then bam kristen comes  to you screaming cut to black  considering the film's nihilistic  attitude kristin's survival is initially  surprising but upon closer inspection  the assailants leaving her alive gives  us some disturbing insight into the  killer's psyche they are not concerned  with the act of killing or even being  caught all that interests them is the  violence rhyme  reason or consequence be damned  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

